### What is equivalence partitioning?

Equivalence partitioning is the foundation of test-design techniques. You divide each input into partitions, ranges or categories that the system treats identically, and then test just one representative value from each partition. If the code handles one member of a class correctly, it almost certainly handles the rest the same way.

For a transfer amount valid between one and one hundred thousand rupees, the partitions are: below range, in range, and above range, plus invalid types such as negative or non-numeric.

```java
// EquivalencePartitioningTest.java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.ValueSource;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class EquivalencePartitioningTest {

    private final TransferValidator validator = new TransferValidator();

    // Partition 1: Below range (invalid)
    @Test
    void zeroAmountIsRejected() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(BigDecimal.ZERO));
    }

    // Partition 2: In range (valid) - one representative
    @Test
    void midRangeAmountIsAccepted() {
        assertTrue(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("50000.00")));
    }

    // Partition 3: Above range (invalid)
    @Test
    void aboveMaximumIsRejected() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("200000.00")));
    }

    // Partition 4: Negative (invalid)
    @Test
    void negativeAmountIsRejected() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("-100.00")));
    }
}
```

### What is boundary value analysis?

Boundary value analysis complements partitioning by attacking the edges, because off-by-one errors and wrong comparison operators cluster exactly at boundaries. For the one-to-one-hundred-thousand range, you test the just-outside and just-inside values.

```java
// BoundaryValueTest.java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class BoundaryValueTest {

    private final TransferValidator validator = new TransferValidator();

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "Amount {0} -> valid={1}")
    @CsvSource({
        "0.00,   false",   // just below min
        "0.99,   false",   // just below min
        "1.00,   true",    // exact min boundary
        "1.01,   true",    // just above min
        "99999.99, true",  // just below max
        "100000.00, true", // exact max boundary
        "100000.01, false", // just above max
        "100001.00, false"  // above max
    })
    void boundaryAmountsAreValidatedCorrectly(
            String amount, boolean expected) {
        assertEquals(expected,
            validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal(amount)));
    }
}
```

```text
  Boundary diagram for transfer amount:

  INVALID   │  VALID RANGE        │  INVALID
  ──────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────
      0.99  │ 1.00  ...  100000  │ 100000.01
            │  ^              ^   │
            │  min boundary   max boundary
            │                     │
  Bugs cluster HERE at the boundaries
```

### What is a decision table?

Decision tables handle combinational logic where the output depends on several conditions. You list each condition as a row, enumerate the combinations as columns, and record the expected action. This both generates test cases and reveals missing or contradictory rules.

```java
// DecisionTableLoanApprovalTest.java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class DecisionTableLoanApprovalTest {

    private final LoanApprovalService service = new LoanApprovalService();

    // Decision table:
    // Income >= 50k | Credit >= 700 | Age >= 21 | Result
    // T             | T             | T          | APPROVED
    // T             | T             | F          | REJECTED (underage)
    // T             | F             | T          | REJECTED (credit)
    // F             | T             | T          | REJECTED (income)
    // F             | F             | T          | REJECTED (income+credit)
    // F             | F             | F          | REJECTED (all fail)

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "Income={0} Credit={1} Age={2} -> {3}")
    @CsvSource({
        "60000, 750, 25, APPROVED",
        "60000, 750, 18, REJECTED",
        "60000, 600, 25, REJECTED",
        "30000, 750, 25, REJECTED",
        "30000, 600, 25, REJECTED",
        "30000, 600, 18, REJECTED",
    })
    void loanApprovalDecisionTable(
            int income, int credit, int age, String expected) {
        LoanApplication app = new LoanApplication(income, credit, age);
        assertEquals(expected, service.evaluate(app).getStatus());
    }
}
```

### What is state transition testing?

State transition testing applies when an entity has a lifecycle. You model the states, the events that cause transitions, and design cases to cover each legal transition plus attempts at illegal transitions to confirm they are rejected.

```java
// LoanStateTransitionTest.java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class LoanStateTransitionTest {

    @Test
    void legalTransition_applied_to_approved() {
        Loan loan = new Loan("LOAN-001", LoanState.APPLIED);
        loan.approve();
        assertEquals(LoanState.APPROVED, loan.getState());
    }

    @Test
    void legalTransition_approved_to_disbursed() {
        Loan loan = new Loan("LOAN-001", LoanState.APPROVED);
        loan.disburse();
        assertEquals(LoanState.DISBURSED, loan.getState());
    }

    @Test
    void illegalTransition_applied_cannot_disburse() {
        Loan loan = new Loan("LOAN-001", LoanState.APPLIED);
        assertThrows(IllegalStateException.class, loan::disburse);
    }

    @Test
    void illegalTransition_closed_cannot_disburse() {
        Loan loan = new Loan("LOAN-001", LoanState.CLOSED);
        assertThrows(IllegalStateException.class, loan::disburse);
    }
}
```

### What is pairwise testing?

Pairwise or combinatorial testing covers all pairs of parameter values with far fewer cases than the full cross-product. Most defects are triggered by the interaction of just two parameters, so covering all pairs catches the large majority of combination bugs.

```java
// PairwiseTransferTest.java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;

public class PairwiseTransferTest {

    // 3 parameters x 3 values each = 27 full combinations
    // Pairwise: only 9 cases cover all pairs
    // Generated by a pairwise tool (e.g., PICT)

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "Account={0} Currency={1} Channel={2}")
    @CsvSource({
        "SAVINGS,  INR, WEB",
        "SAVINGS,  USD, MOBILE",
        "SAVINGS,  EUR, API",
        "CURRENT,  INR, MOBILE",
        "CURRENT,  USD, API",
        "CURRENT,  EUR, WEB",
        "NRI,      INR, API",
        "NRI,      USD, WEB",
        "NRI,      EUR, MOBILE",
    })
    void pairwiseTransferCombinations(
            String accountType, String currency, String channel) {
        // Each pair of values appears at least once
        // 9 cases instead of 27
        TransferResult result = transferService.transfer(
            accountType, currency, channel,
            new java.math.BigDecimal("1000.00")
        );
        // Assert based on expected behavior for this combination
    }
}
```

### How do these techniques work together?

These techniques are complementary, not alternatives. You choose based on the feature's characteristics.

| Technique | Use When | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Equivalence partitioning | Input has ranges or categories | Transfer amount: below, valid, above |
| Boundary value analysis | Edges of partitions matter | 0.99, 1.00, 100000, 100000.01 |
| Decision table | Multiple conditions combine | Loan approval: income + credit + age |
| State transition | Entity has a lifecycle | Loan: applied, approved, disbursed, closed |
| Pairwise | Many parameters would explode | Account type x currency x channel |

```text
  Workflow: choosing the right technique

  Feature has ranges?  ──> Equivalence Partitioning
       │                        + Boundary Value Analysis
       │
  Feature has combined ──> Decision Table
  conditions?
       │
  Feature has lifecycle ──> State Transition Testing
  states?
       │
  Feature has many     ──> Pairwise / Combinatorial
  independent params?
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is applying only one technique. Partitioning alone misses boundary bugs. Boundaries alone miss combination bugs. A good tester layers the techniques.

The second mistake is creating partitions that do not reflect how the system actually behaves. If the system treats amounts above fifty thousand differently from amounts above one hundred thousand, you need both thresholds as partition boundaries, not just one.

```text
Wrong: One partition for "valid range" 1 to 100000
       Misses: system applies different fees at 50000

Correct: Three partitions for valid range
         1-49999 (standard fee)
         50000-99999 (reduced fee)
         100000 (maximum, special handling)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How many test cases does boundary value analysis produce?" For each boundary: the boundary value, one below, and one above. For a range with two boundaries, that is six cases.
- "When would you use a decision table instead of pairwise?" When the conditions are dependent and produce different actions based on specific combinations. Pairwise is for independent parameters.
- "Can you combine these techniques?" Always. Partition first to shrink the space, apply boundaries to the edges, use a decision table for combined rules, and use pairwise if parameters remain.
